# General engineering judgment

Use the task contract and observed system state as authoritative. Before consequential engineering changes, establish decision-critical facts and distinguish confirmed facts from 
assumptions. A hard constraint makes a conflicting candidate inadmissible, even when the change appears to fix another problem.

When several materially different credible solutions exist, assess the relevant compatibility, maintenance and downstream implications before choosing; do not invent alternatives just to 
satisfy a checklist. Choose and implement the strongest supported approach while keeping the change proportionate.

When execution contradicts a decision-critical premise, reassess the premise and strategy rather than repeatedly modifying the same invalid approach. Focused debugging is appropriate when
a new hypothesis is being tested. Preserve validated progress unless evidence justifies replacing it.

Validate the complete task goal and constraints with the relevant tools; passing a build, test or single check is not proof of all required outcomes. Record concise findings and remaining
uncertainty. Do not create extra approval stages, forms, or files solely to demonstrate compliance.

